+
+## Agents
+
+### The `sys_link_rules` tool
+
+Agents can read and change the rules with `sys_link_rules`:
+
+| `action` | Arguments | Effect |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `list` | none | Returns the rules in effect and whether they are saved |
+| `add` | `name`, then `kind` (default `url`), `pattern` and `url` or `provider` and `host`, `enabled` (default true) | Appends a rule |
+| `update` | `name`, then any of `new_name`, `kind`, `pattern`, `url`, `provider`, `host`, `enabled` | Changes one rule |
+| `remove` | `name` | Deletes one rule |
+
+Names match case-insensitively. The tool checks the changed list with the
+server's validator before saving it, and saves it with the version it read, so
+a change made meanwhile from Settings is refused rather than overwritten.
+
+Every `add`, `update` and `remove` parks on an approval prompt in the session,
+showing the rule's name, pattern, URL, provider and host, even when no other policy applies
+(the built-in `__ask_on_link_rule_change` policy, like the one for scheduled
+tasks). `list` is not gated. Agents get the tool unless their spec sets
+`tool_groups.link_rules: false` ([agent YAML](AGENT_YAML_SPEC.md#tool-groups)),
+and native Claude and Codex sessions get it through the tool relay.
+
+### What agents are told
+
+Agents are told which references the chat links, so they write them as plain
+text instead of code: one short framework instruction listing each enabled
+`url` and `session` rule's syntax, such as `` `ABC-123` (Jira) ``. With every
+rule off there is no instruction.
+
+When the session's workspace is a git checkout (a `.git` directory or file in
+it or an ancestor), a second sentence asks for `issue #123` or `PR #123`
+rather than a bare `#123`, and for `owner/repo#123` for another repository.
+It names only the notations that link in that session: the provider's own
+`!123` only when the workspace's remote is on a host whose `git` rule uses it
+(GitLab, Azure DevOps), and `owner/repo#123` only when the GitHub rule is on
+or the session's provider links it. The runner picks the rule as the chat
+does, from the provider and host of the workspace's first remote. Outside a
+checkout, or with every `git` rule off, there is no such sentence.
+
+A rule saved mid-session renders in the chat right away. SDK harnesses read
+the rules again for a later turn (the runner keeps them, and each workspace's
+remote, for up to a minute).
+Native Claude and Codex sessions get their instructions once, at launch, so
+they see a changed rule in their prompt only after their next launch.
